## Grammar rules

1. Use two-digit decade names at the top level (`10_documents`) and `NN.MM_`
   names at the second level (`10.01_personal`). Numbering stops after these
   two levels; deeper directories use plain names. `99_tmp` is the scratch
   exception to decade numbering.
2. At numbered levels, use ASCII lowercase and underscores. Leaf content may
   keep its original names, including spaces and non-ASCII characters.
3. Never rename numbered directories or recycle their numbers. Record retired
   categories in `00_meta/RETIRED.md`; their numbers stay reserved permanently.
   That log is operator-maintained and is never overwritten by the role.
4. At the two numbered levels, numbered directories belong to the preserved
   human taxonomy and are intended for parity inclusion; unnumbered lowercase
   directories are machine-owned and parity-excluded. Deeper plain names
   inherit their ancestor's tier. `99_tmp` is deliberately excluded from parity
   and backups. `20_photos/20.02_camera` is machine-fed but parity-included:
   camera dumps may be the sole copy until promotion. `70_backups` contains
   machine-generated archives; keep their restoration metadata and do not
   hand-edit their contents. Parity protection requires a successful SnapRAID
   sync; this legend describes the intended policy, not proof of protection.
5. Service shares alias numbers away (`media` maps to `30_media`, `photos` to
   `20_photos`). Services mount only their assigned subtree.

## Machines feed, humans promote

`90_inbox` is the human's unsorted inbox, intended for parity inclusion.
`10_documents/inbox` is a machine-consumed drop directory, excluded from parity.
`99_tmp` is disposable staging and validation scratch.

The media machine tier is entirely under `30_media/rotation`: Transmission
feeds `downloads/{complete,incomplete}`, and the media managers hardlink into
`movies` or `shows`. Each linking container needs both paths under one mount.
Only humans promote keepers into the numbered media directories; automation
must not delete or replace preserved media. Jellyfin serves media read-only.

For photos, `20.02_camera` is the writable triage tier and `20.01_library` is
the preserved archive, mounted read-only by Immich. Promote within a NAS share
through one operator NFS mount containing both paths, with competing activity
for the item paused. For uploads held on Ceph, verify the copied content before
deleting the source. Cryptomator containers in `80_vault` stay opaque; never
decrypt them server-side.

## The working-set test for 50_topics

Use `50_topics` only when splitting a collection by medium would destroy its
value as a working set: D&D rulebooks, character sheets, tools and scenarios
belong together, as do an airsoft collection's manuals, scenarios and photos.
A file valuable on its own belongs with its medium: a yoga book goes to
`40_library/40.01_books`, a guitar course to `40_library/40.02_courses`.
Add topic categories deliberately, not merely because several files share a
subject.
